// Zulu: the black palmered loch fly with a red tail.

id zulu
name "Zulu"
aliases "Black Zulu"
type wet
imitates attractor
difficulty beginner
species trout sea-trout char
season april may june july august september
origin region="Scotland, by 1882"
description "A short red wool tail, a black seal's fur body ribbed with fine oval silver, and a black cock hackle palmered from tail to head."
history """
    Nobody knows who tied the first. By 1882 "Black Palmer" could already list "black spiders
    with red tips, commonly called Zulus" among the flies for a fortnight on remote Scottish
    lochs, and in 1892 Niven's lexicon described it as "made like a palmer, black hackle, black
    body, ribbed silver tinsel, with red wool tag for tail". Walbran's 1889 catalogue, as Viking
    Lars reports it, gives it a silver tag and hen hackles. Two modern sources say it was once
    banned, from competitions or in some waters, for catching too well; neither gives details.
    """
fishing """
    A loch fly, at its best on the bob: the top dropper of a team of three or four, worked
    through the wave tops from a drifting boat. Dark, windy days suit it. It takes brown trout
    and sea trout all over Britain and Ireland, and brown trout and Arctic char in Iceland. A
    letter to the Fishing Gazette in 1893 recommended it for Donegal sea trout "dressed small,
    but with long hackle and with plenty of tinsel".
    """
sources {
    "Internet Archive: Scotch Loch-Fishing (Black Palmer, 1882)" url="https://archive.org/details/scotchloch-fishi00senirich"
    "Internet Archive: The British Angler's Lexicon (Richard Niven, 1892)" url="https://archive.org/details/britishanglersle00niverich"
    "Sexyloops: (Black) Zulu (Viking Lars)" url="https://www.sexyloops.com/index.php/ps/black-zulu"
    "Trout & Salmon Fishing: Trout Flies, Traditional Scottish Flies" url="https://www.trout-salmon-fishing.com/trout-flies.htm"
    "Sexyloops: Zulu (fly fishing glossary, Ben Spinks)" url="https://www.sexyloops.com/glossary/zulu.shtml"
    "GlobalFlyFisher: Blue Zulu (Davie McPhail)" url="https://globalflyfisher.com/video/blue-zulu"
    "GlobalFlyFisher: Black Zulu (Ivar's Fly Workshop)" url="https://globalflyfisher.com/video/black-zulu-0"
    "Internet Archive: The Fishing Gazette, vol. 26 (1893)" url="https://archive.org/details/fishinggazettede26unse"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (npm run refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://www.trout-salmon-fishing.com/Zulu.jpg" page="https://www.trout-salmon-fishing.com/trout-flies.htm" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view"
    "https://www.fish4flies.com/img/flies/Original/854-854.jpg" page="https://www.fish4flies.com/Wet/Hackled/Black-Zulu" license="All rights reserved; reference only" flip=#true note="Side view"
}

hook wet-standard size=12
thread black size="8/0"

materials {
    tail wool color=red
    rib oval-tinsel color=silver label="Fine oval silver tinsel"
    hackle cock-hackle color=black fibre=1.3 sweep=40
    body seals-fur color=black
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.88
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    // Assumption: about a third to half the shank past the bend, as in modern photos.
    tie-in tail length=0.9 spread=55 note="A short tuft, on top."
    tie-in rib
    tie-in hackle note="Shiny side forward."
    dub body to=0.86 note="Slightly tapered; pick it out a little."
    wind hackle to=0.86 turns=6 note="Open turns, tail to head."
    tie-off hackle
    wind rib to=0.86 turns=5 note="Counter-wound through the hackle, crossing the stem."
    tie-off rib
    whip-finish
}
